# Package an application, then run it. This is the one test that exercises the
# whole feature the way a user does: a directory in, an executable out, and
# that executable started from somewhere that is not the application directory
# and told nothing about where it came from. Everything else in Packaging/
# tests a piece. Every RUN line runs from lit's output directory, which is
# deliberately not the fixture: an application that only starts next to its
# own source files has not packaged anything.
#
# A pass proves more than "it exited 0": run_bundle sets
# require_script_images, so the launcher refuses to start if any of the page's
# scripts had to be compiled from source, a failure nothing else would report.
# The negative cases are the second half: the same launcher with nothing
# appended, handed an HTML file, or handed the two bundles ctcompile will not
# produce but a future packager might (ctcompile-test-app_bundle writes them)
# must all refuse. That guard is in run_app, behind a window, and nothing else
# in the suite gets that far.
#
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t && ctcompile-test-app_bundle %t
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --launcher ctrun -o %t/app --allow-env-overrides --verbose --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --manifest %t/app.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=PACKAGED
# RUN: test -x %t/app
#
# --allow-env-overrides is needed because the runs below bound themselves with
# CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES: without it a packaged application ignores every
# CTBROWSER_* variable (unittests/unit/navigation.cpp holds that), and a loose
# .ctapp handed to ctrun reads them whatever it was packaged with.
#
# The --verbose report is checked by its numbers rather than its phrasing. Two
# scripts is the count that matters, because a packager that quietly packed
# one produces an application that starts and is refused for a reason pointing
# at the launcher. greeting.js and app.css are named by the document, not by
# the command line, and late.json is the one a script asked for after the page
# was up: fetch queues its request and it is drained from a tick, so this name
# exists only if the packager let the page run before asking what it wanted.
# That is the difference between packaging a document and an application.
# PACKAGED-DAG: 2 scripts compiled
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  greeting.js
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  app.css
# PACKAGED-DAG: asset  late.json
#
# Opening a file is not proof that its bytes reached the destination. Linux's
# full device deterministically rejects the write/close while permitting open.
# RUN: not ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --bundle -o /dev/full 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FULL
# RUN: not ctcompile %S/app --entry index.html --fonts %{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts --bundle -o %t/failed.ctapp --manifest /dev/full 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FULL
# FULL: cannot write{{.*}}/dev/full
#
# The manifest is parsed rather than matched: a grep for a field name would
# pass on a document no parser accepts, so json.tool reads it first and
# FileCheck reads json.tool's normalised output. The fonts are under a name of
# the compiler's own, not the build machine's path, and every program id is
# distinct, because two scripts sharing one could not be told apart at lookup.
# RUN: python3 -m json.tool %t/app.json | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MANIFEST
# MANIFEST-DAG: "entry": "index.html"
# MANIFEST-DAG: "font_directory": "fonts"
# RUN: python3 -c 'import json, sys; s = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))["scripts"]; sys.exit(len(s) != 2 or s[0]["program_id"] == s[1]["program_id"])' %t/app.json
# RUN: test $(stat -c %%s %t/app) -gt $(stat -c %%s ctrun)
#
# ---- run it, from somewhere that is not the application directory ----------
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 %t/app
# --info prints the packaged application's manifest and does not start the
# application.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy %t/app --info | python3 -m json.tool | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=INFO
# INFO: "entry": "index.html"
# --help answers the same way. Every other argument is ignored, never refused:
# an application cannot take arguments of its own yet, so none may stop it
# starting; a stray flag and a bare word must still run it.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy %t/app --help | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=HELP
# HELP: packaged by ctcompile
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 %t/app --not-a-flag stray
#
# ---- the negative cases ----------------------------------------------------
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %S/app/index.html 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NOT-A-BUNDLE
# NOT-A-BUNDLE: not a ctbrowser application bundle
#
# ---- ctbrowse's own flags ---------------------------------------------------
# --size, --frames and --shot, which the renders below set through the
# CTBROWSER_* variables instead: the screenshot is the window's size.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctbrowse %S/app/index.html --size 320 240 --frames 2 --shot %t/flags.ppm
# RUN: head -c 15 %t/flags.ppm | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FLAGS
# FLAGS: P6
# FLAGS-NEXT: 320 240
#
# ---- and it must look the same as the page it came from ---------------------
# THE CHECK THAT CAUGHT THE FONTS. A packaged application is sealed - it answers
# from what it carries and never from the disk - and the vendored fonts are
# loaded through the asset registry; if packaging drops them the application
# silently falls back to the built-in bitmap font, exits 0 and just looks
# worse. So the same page is rendered twice with real fonts, once from source
# with CTBROWSER_FONT_PATH and once packaged with no font path and a working
# directory that is not the application's (the only way this can see the
# bundle doing the work), and the bytes are compared. It is deliberately last,
# because a byte comparison of two renders catches anything the packaging
# lost, not only fonts. Without TTF support both renders fall back to the
# bitmap font and the comparison proves less.
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONT_PATH=%{monorepo}/ctbrowser/resources/fonts CTBROWSER_SCREENSHOT=%t/from-source.ppm ctbrowse %S/app/index.html
# RUN: env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_SCREENSHOT=%t/packaged.ppm %t/app
# RUN: cmp %t/from-source.ppm %t/packaged.ppm
#
# ---- what the compiler must refuse to package -------------------------------
# A page of module scripts. Nothing here can compile one ahead of time, so
# packaging it would produce an application exactly as slow as before, with
# every count in the packager reading a truthful zero: the silent failure this
# tool exists to avoid.
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctcompile %S/app-module --entry index.html --launcher ctrun -o %t/app-module 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=MODULE
# MODULE: module script
#
# ---- and what the launcher must refuse to run -------------------------------
# RUN: test -f %t/no-images.ctapp && test -f %t/module-page.ctapp
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %t/no-images.ctapp
# RUN: not env SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy CTBROWSER_TEST_FRAMES=2 CTBROWSER_NETWORK=0 CTBROWSER_FONTS=font8x8 ctrun %t/module-page.ctapp
